IMHO this mapping to lb: "yn" most probably is based on a
misunderstanding, maybe going back to pronunciation standards in=20
southern Chinese dialects like Cantonese: Mandarin (pinyin) -eng (lb:
-yn(g)) very often is pron. -ong (=3Dung) or -un in=20
southern dialects (e.g. BIG5 =C2=D7 pinyin: feng1, "fruitful/abundant"
in dialect is *written* and pronounced "fung" - pron. in=20
'virtual' pinyin about: fong -, or BIG5 =AD=B7 pinyin: feng1, "wind"
etc. in dialect written and pron. "fun" as in Cantonese taifun, BIG5=20
=A4j=AD=B7, pinyin: dafeng!)=20
Pinyin "-ong" should be transcribed to lb: "un(g)" *not* -yn (e.g.
mau.dzydun.)

> The g is questionable because Lojban maps the /ng/ consonant to=20
> /n/.  As someone noted, if the g is present it is pronounced
separately=20
> from the n.  But the real problem is that in gismu making we could
have=20
> ended up with the g and not the n in the Lojban word, and the g by
